Same idea as normal BFS.

Instead of: level[node]
we use: int[][] dist = new int[n][m];

Code:

int[][] dist = new int[n][m];
boolean[][] visited = new boolean[n][m];
Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();
q.add(new int[]{sr, sc});

visited[sr][sc] = true;
dist[sr][sc] = 0;

int[] dr = {-1, 1, 0, 0};
int[] dc = {0, 0, -1, 1};

while(!q.isEmpty()) {

    int[] cell = q.poll();

    int r = cell[0];
    int c = cell[1];

    for(int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        int nr = r + dr[i];
        int nc = c + dc[i];

        if(nr < 0 || nr >= n ||
           nc < 0 || nc >= m) {
            continue;
        }

        if(visited[nr][nc]) {
            continue;
        }

        visited[nr][nc] = true;
        dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + 1;
        q.add(new int[]{nr, nc});
    }
}

Key line dist[nr][nc] = dist[r][c] + 1;
Exactly equivalent to: level[neighbour] = level[node] + 1;
